-- v8 -> v9: retire the `UPLOADED` state (capability `photo-sharing`).
--
-- `UPLOADED` meant "the bytes are stored, and the work a completion triggers has not run". That work is gone
-- from the completion path — the device manifest is the announcement, and own-photo album placement happens
-- when the upload is first enqueued — so a successful upload is now recorded `COMPLETED` directly, and an
-- `UPLOADED` row records exactly what `COMPLETED` does.
--
-- DATA-ONLY, the first ledger migration that is: no schema changes, so the SQLDelight verify task (migrated
-- schema vs created schema) cannot tell a wrong rewrite from a right one. The migration test in
-- `LedgerServiceTest` is what asserts it. That remains true now that the verify task actually
-- compares something (the schema snapshot, see Ledger.sq's header): it gained the power to catch schema
-- drift and still has none over a row rewrite, so this file's coverage is the test and only the test.
--
-- REQUIRED, not tidy-up. `state` decodes through an enum that no longer names `UPLOADED`, and the state-scoped
-- reads compare stored text against bound state sets — so an unrewritten row would either fail to decode or
-- count as pending forever, with no error anywhere.
--
-- A STATE REWRITE ONLY. It places nothing in an event album (SQL cannot reach PhotoKit, and a backfill that
-- re-places a library on first launch is the failure the `UPLOADED` state's own decision record warned
-- against) and creates no upload work: a converted row is simply settled.
--
-- DOWNGRADE: the native driver refuses a database newer than the binary's schema, so a revert of this change
-- keeps this file and reverts only the Kotlin. Re-applying the change after such a revert needs a further
-- migration carrying the same rewrite, because this one has already run.
--
-- Decision record: `changes/retire-uploaded-state` (D3, D4).
UPDATE ledgerRow SET state = 'COMPLETED' WHERE state = 'UPLOADED';
